lary at that step. Also given is an integer `beam_width` (k). Return the top\u2011k most likely sequences (as lists of tokens) together with their log\u2011probability scores. Use natural logarithm for scores. Example:\n```\nprob_distributions = [\n    [(\'A\',0.6),(\'B\',0.4)],\n    [(\'A\',0.3),(\'B\',0.7)],\n    [(\'A\',0.5),(\'B\',0.5)]\n]\nbeam_width = 2\n```\nThe function should return something like:\n```\n[([\'A\',\'B\',\'A\'], -1.2039728), ([\'A\',\'B\',\'B\'], -1.2039728)]\n```\n(Exact ordering may vary if scores tie). The algorithm must handle any number of time steps, any vocabulary size, and any reasonable beam width. Return an empty list if `prob_distributions` is empty.\n\nCompetency being assessed: algorithms\nStarter code (if any): def beam_search_decoder(prob_distributions, beam_width):\n    """Return top\u2011k sequences and their log\u2011prob scores.\n    Args:\n        prob_distributions: List[List[Tuple[str,float]]] – length T, each inner list contains (token, prob).\n        beam_width: int – number of beams to keep.\n    Returns:\n        List[Tuple[List[str], float]] – each tuple is (sequence, log_score).\n    """\n    # Your implementation here\n    pass\n\nSupported language(s): python, javascript\nConstraints: 1 <= len(prob_distributions) <= 1000\n1 <= beam_width <= 100\nEach probability is a float in [0,1] and sums to 1 per time step.\nVocabulary size per step <= 1000.\n\n\nFIRST TURN for this question (no candidate answer yet):\nPresent the problem directly and naturally, mention any given constraints,\nand invite the candidate to think aloud and walk you through their approach\nbefore or while they write code. Use action=ASK.\n\nDURING SOLVING (this is a live discussion, not silent grading):\n- The candidate thinks aloud and may discuss their approach with you at any\n  point — listen, acknowledge, and ask genuine clarifying questions about\n  THEIR approach if something is unclear. Do NOT use action=FOLLOW_UP to\n  probe further once